Hecks.hecksagon "Conductor" do
  # pr-delivery-merge-on-accept (sprint 20), completed i787. The REAL merge
  # leg: squash-merge the current story's PR into main and delete the
  # branch. Re-expressed from pr_delivery.hecksagon's 3rd binding, a bare
  # `run "gh pr merge sq/{story_ref} --squash --delete-branch"` driven-block
  # leaf -- a confirmed hard no-op in the vendored gem (`DrivenCapture
  # #run(_cmd, result_into: nil) = nil` is an explicit stub), so this merge
  # has never once actually executed -- onto the proven spawned_by
  # mechanism (i780's design, i781/i782's E2E-proven pattern).
  #
  # MUTATING, and the sharpest of the five `run` sites i782 surveyed:
  # landing this turns on automation that merges real PRs. Converted with
  # Chris's explicit sign-off (i782 -> /goal execute 1-3).
  #
  # WHY IT LIVES IN conductor/, NOT plan/. The old leaf sat in
  # plan/hecksagons/pr_delivery.hecksagon, but the triggering event is
  # Conductor::MergeQueue.MergeStarted and the verdict targets are
  # MergeQueue's own commands. A spawned_by bind is declared in the domain
  # that owns the aggregate -- the convention every already-proven
  # conversion follows (Conductor::Lease in conductor/bluebook/
  # worktree_create.hecksagon, Plan::Story in plan/bluebook/
  # pr_mergeable_check.hecksagon). It also has to live DIRECTLY in
  # bluebook/: i782 established empirically that a sibling hecksagons/
  # directory is never globbed by Folder#load_domain at any boot depth, so
  # a bind declared there is structurally invisible and silently does
  # nothing.
  #
  # MergeQueue.identified_by :branch -- the queue's id IS the target branch
  # ("main"), so adapter-host's automatic id=<source_id> on the verdict
  # dispatch already addresses the right record. bin/pr_delivery_merge
  # reads `story_ref` off the triggering event's payload to build the
  # sq/<story_ref> branch name.
  #
  # success/failure are DIFFERENT commands here, unlike the check
  # conversions where one command records either outcome via its own :ok.
  # A merge that failed must still return the queue to idle -- otherwise
  # the next story can never start -- and FailMerge is what says so.
  # Both were widened (i787, merge_queue.bluebook) to accept adapter-host's
  # unconditional id/tool/output/exit_code/ok verdict shape, with every
  # non-addressing attribute optional, because adapter-host layers a
  # handler's extra fields onto the verdict ONLY on the success path: a
  # required attribute on FailMerge could never be delivered, and would
  # wedge the queue in `state: merging` forever the first time `gh pr
  # merge` genuinely failed. That delivery asymmetry is the exact reason
  # i782 stopped short of converting this binding.
  Conductor::MergeQueue.spawned_by("PrDeliveryMerge", on: "MergeStarted")
end
